It is found in foods such as liver (but avoid this during pregnancy), red meat, beans (including red kidney beans, edamame beans and chickpeas), nuts, fortified breakfast cereals, dried fruit (such as dried apricots), dark‑green leafy vegetables (e.g., watercress and curly kale), cereals and bread fortified with iron, meat, dried fruit (e.g., apricots, prunes and raisins), and pulses (beans, peas and lentils) (https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet; source: NHS).  
The recommended daily amount for adults is 8.7 mg per day for men aged 19 years and over, 14.8 mg per day for women aged 19 to 49 years, and 8.7 mg per day for women aged 50 years and over (https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet; source: NHS).  
The sources do not specify any required action after accidental ingestion of iron tablets; they only note that doses over 20 mg per day may cause side effects such as constipation, nausea, vomiting, and stomach pain (http://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron; source: NHS).  

